course: adult-life
lesson: 25
title: Funerals and remembering
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A funeral is a ceremony where family and friends say goodbye to someone who has died. They share memories, sing or pray, and then the body is buried or cremated.
explain: Picture a small church in Wellington: at the front stands a long wooden box covered in flowers, the coffin. Afterwards everyone goes to the pub, and some people even laugh while telling stories about Grandad.
explain: Remembering does not stop after the funeral. Families visit graves, send cards, mark the anniversary, hold memorial services and even put up benches with little plaques.
explain: Children are usually welcome at funerals, and it is their choice whether to go. Asking what will happen first makes it less strange.
fact: A funeral is a ceremony to say goodbye to someone who has died; people often dress in dark colours, and many cry.
fact: Children are usually welcome at funerals; many say later they were glad they went, and a grown-up should look after them during it.
fact: A wake is a gathering after a funeral where people eat, drink and share stories; laughing there does not mean nobody is sad.
fact: The coffin is the long box that holds the body of the person who died; it is often closed, and it goes to the grave or the crematorium.
fact: Cremation means the body is burned in a crematorium until only ashes are left; the dead person cannot feel anything.
fact: After a cremation the ashes are given to the family in an urn and may be buried, kept or scattered somewhere the person loved.
fact: Burial means the coffin is put into a grave in the ground and covered with earth; a headstone with the name is added later.
fact: Many families visit graves to remember someone, tidy the spot and leave flowers; talking to the person there is normal.
fact: The anniversary of a death often brings the sadness back strongly; families may light a candle, visit the grave or share stories.
fact: A sympathy card is sent to someone whose loved one has died; lilies are flowers linked with funerals, and short kind words mean a lot.
fact: A memorial service remembers someone who has died, usually without the coffin, and may be months after the funeral; it is often more about celebrating the life.
fact: Families sometimes pay for a bench, tree or plaque in a place the person loved; sitting on a memorial bench is fine, that is what it is for.
exercise: "The funeral is next Friday. You don't have to come if you don't want to." What is a funeral? | A ceremony to say goodbye to someone who has died | A party to celebrate someone's birthday | A meeting at the bank about money | A school concert in memory of the summer holidays and all the fun
exercise: Are children allowed at funerals? | Usually yes, and it is their choice whether to go | Never, funerals are only for adults and older teenagers | Only if they are older than sixteen | Only if they promise not to cry
exercise: You decide to go to the funeral. What helps most? | Asking a grown-up what will happen beforehand | Bringing a football to play with outside the church | Not telling anyone you are scared | Wearing your brightest party clothes
exercise: "Everyone's going back to the pub after the service." What is this gathering called? | A wake | A christening | A reunion | A shower
exercise: At the wake, people laugh while telling stories about Grandad. What does it mean? | Remembering happy times is part of saying goodbye | Nobody at the wake is sad about Grandad any more | The grown-ups are being very rude | They are glad that Grandad has died
exercise: At the front of the church is a long wooden box with flowers on top. What is it? | The coffin, which holds the body | A box of presents for the family | A table for the hymn books | A cupboard for the flowers
exercise: "She's going to be cremated." What does cremated mean? | Her body will be burned until only ashes are left | She will be put to sleep in a special bed for ever and ever | Her body will be given to a museum | She will be buried at sea from a ship
exercise: Does a dead person feel anything during cremation? | No, a dead person cannot feel anything | Yes, but only a little bit of warmth | Yes, which is why it is done very quickly | Only if they were not given an anaesthetic
exercise: After a cremation, what happens to the ashes? | They go to the family, who may bury, keep or scatter them | They are always thrown away by the crematorium | They are given to the nearest hospital | They are put in the coffin with the flowers
exercise: "He's being buried in the churchyard next to Nan." What does buried mean? | The coffin is put into a grave in the ground | The body is burned and the ashes kept in a jar at home | The body is taken to another country | The coffin is kept inside the church
exercise: What is added to a grave later, with the person's name on it? | A headstone | A letterbox | A signpost | A bench
exercise: On Sundays you put fresh flowers on Grandad's grave. Why do families do this? | To remember him and keep him close | Because the council asks them to every week | To win a prize for the nicest grave | Because Grandad can smell the flowers
exercise: Mum says, "It's a year today since Grandpa died." Why might she be quiet that day? | Anniversaries often bring the sadness back strongly | She is cross that nobody remembered her birthday this year | She has a sore throat and cannot talk | She has forgotten who Grandpa was
exercise: A card with lilies on it says "With deepest sympathy". Who is it for? | Someone whose loved one has died | Someone who has just had a baby | Someone who has passed an exam | Someone who is moving house
exercise: "There'll be a memorial service later in the year." How is it different from a funeral? | It is usually without the coffin and often celebrates the life | It is always held before the person has died, so they can hear it | It is a funeral held only for the children | It is a party where nobody mentions the person
exercise: A park bench has a plaque: "In loving memory of Joan, who loved this view". May you sit on it? | Yes, that is what it is for | No, only Joan's family may sit there | No, it must be kept clean for Joan | Only on Joan's birthday each year
test: Your brother says, "I'm not going to the funeral, I'll get in trouble." What is true? | It is his choice, and he will not be in trouble either way | He must go, or he will be punished by the family | He cannot go because children are not allowed | He must go and must not show any feelings
test: Auntie on the phone: "Service at eleven, then back to the Red Lion for sandwiches." What is happening at the Red Lion? | A wake after the funeral | A birthday party for a cousin | A meeting about the house | A lunch for the church choir
test: Your cousin whispers, "Everyone's laughing at the wake, they don't even care." How do you put him right? | Sharing happy stories is a way of remembering, not a sign they don't care | He is right, laughing means nobody is sad | They are laughing because the funeral is over | Grown-ups are not allowed to be sad at a wake
test: Your sister asks, "What's in that box at the front, is Nan in there?" What is the honest answer? | Yes, it is the coffin and Nan's body is inside | No, it is a box of Nan's old clothes for charity | No, it is a box of flowers for the grave | Yes, and Nan is asleep inside it
test: Dad on the phone: "We're scattering his ashes on the beach where he fished." What happened before? | His body was cremated | His body was buried | He went to the beach to fish | He moved house to the seaside
test: Your friend says, "Cremation hurts, that's why I'd rather be buried." What is wrong with this? | A dead person cannot feel anything at all | Nothing, cremation is very painful | Burial hurts much more than cremation | Only children feel anything when cremated
test: Gran at the churchyard: "Hello, love, the roses are out again." Who is she talking to? | Grandad, at his grave, which is a normal thing to do | A gardener working in the churchyard | Nobody, she is confused and does not know where she is | A neighbour hiding behind the headstone
test: Mum is quiet and sets out Grandpa's old mug one morning. Your brother says, "What's up with her?" What is the likely reason? | It is the anniversary of Grandpa's death | She is cross that the mug was not washed up again | She wants to give the mug away | She is bored and wants a new mug
test: You want to write in a sympathy card to your friend whose mum died. Which is best? | "I'm sorry your mum died. She was kind." | "Cheer up, at least you have your dad." | "Everybody dies, so it's not a big deal." | "Can I have her old bike now?"
test: Dad: "No coffin at the memorial, just photos, his favourite songs and the funny stories." What kind of event is this? | A memorial service celebrating his life | A funeral with a burial in the churchyard | A wake held at the local pub | A wedding for his younger brother
test: A man shoos you off a bench: "That's someone's memorial, show some respect!" Is he right? | No, memorial benches are meant to be sat on | Yes, memorial benches are only for looking at | Yes, only the family may ever sit there | No, because the bench belongs to the council
test: Mum on the phone: "He's going in next to his mum, at St Mary's." What does this mean? | He will be buried beside his mother at St Mary's | He is moving into his mother's house next door to the church | He is going to visit his mother in hospital | He is going to the church to sing in the choir
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a wake? | A gathering following a funeral at which persons eat, drink and share recollections | A religious ceremony conducted prior to the death of the person | A formal hearing at which the will of the deceased is contested | A period of compulsory silence observed by the bereaved
exam: Identify the principal distinction between burial and cremation. | Burial places the coffin in the ground; cremation reduces the body to ashes | Burial is permitted only for children; cremation only for adults | Burial is conducted at sea; cremation is conducted in a churchyard | There exists no distinction whatsoever between the two practices in law or custom
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the attendance of children at funerals. | Children are ordinarily permitted to attend, and their wishes are respected | Children are prohibited by law from attending any funeral | Children are compelled to attend irrespective of their wishes | Children may attend only if they have attained sixteen years
exam: It is given that a plaque reading "In loving memory of" has been affixed to a public bench. Determine the purpose of the said bench. | To commemorate a deceased person, while remaining available for public use | To reserve the seat exclusively for members of the deceased's family and their guests | To mark the location at which the deceased is buried | To indicate that the bench is the property of a church
